[White "4.4) Central Control: Test"]
[Black "Chapter Summary: Key Takeaways"]
[Result "*"]

 { Below is an overview of concepts introduced in this chapter: (1) Defining central control: The side with the superior central control typically has either a dominant pawn formation in the centre, or dominant piece activity, or in many cases both. (2) The link between space & central control: These concepts are very closely linked, but they are not exactly the same. While the side with greater central control will tend to have more space, it is also possible for the reverse to hold true. (3) The centre & attacking: Control of the centre will often open up attacking opportunities, as you are able to enjoy greater piece activity/mobility, as well as being helpful in limiting the opponent’s chances for counterplay. (4) Advancing in the centre: One of the ways in which you can benefit from central control is by pushing pawns. Sometimes the goal of this will be to try to steamroll the opponent (much like in the examples covered in the ‘Space’ chapter). At other times, the objective will be to influence your and your opponent’s piece activity, such as in the case of convergence zones (an important square through which a lot of pieces operate). (5) Fighting against the centre: There are many methods that can be employed in the fight against the centre. Some of the more popular ones include pawn breaks, counterplay along the flanks, targeting the structure, and forcing piece exchanges. (6) Protecting the centre: Similarly, there are also many methods or techniques that can be used to protect the centre when you are the side with the advantage here. These include anticipating pawn breaks, transforming the structure, and using tactics & calculation to maintain or even improve your central control. } 1. { -KEY- }  
 *

