 { Black to play has to be careful because White is threatening h4-h5 next, when the knight will be kicked back to a horribly passive square. Can Black challenge the White central superiority in time? } 1... { -KEY- } f6 $1 2. exf6 gxf6 $11 { Correct! By first going for the pawn break and then taking back with a pawn on f6, Black breaks down the White centre and at the same time keeps control over the critical e5 square. The position is approximately balanced, with White having the more compact structure while Black enjoys the greater central pawn presence. } ( 2... Nxf6 $2 { would lose control over the e5 square, and allow White to dominate the centre by gripping the dark squares. } 3. h5 Nf8 4. Nbd2 { White is much better here, since it is very difficult for Black to play ...e5 and mobilise their pawn majority. } N8d7 { Threatening ...e5, but White is in time to kill this plan. } 5. c4 $16 { The pressure against the d5 pawn stops Black from playing ...e5 any time soon. } ) 

