 { White has a powerful centre, but Black has a typical undermining move at their disposal. Black to play. } 1... { -KEY- } e5 $1 2. dxe5 ( { If } 2. Nf3 { then either } exd4 { or } Bg4 { allows Black to continue to undermine the centre. } ) 
Qxd1+ $1 { Correct! This kind of pawn sacrifice is very scary for most players, since not only are they gambiting a central pawn but the follow-up involves simplifying into a queenless middlegame. However, White has enough holes in their position that Black has full compensation. Play might continue as follows: } 3. Nxd1 Nc6 4. f4 f6 5. exf6 gxf6 6. Nf3 Be6 7. b3 O-O-O 8. Be2 Bc5 $11 { Black is at least equal here, with many open lines to pressure the White position. The inability to castle is a problem for the opponent, while the possibility of ...Nb4 is also hard to meet. } 
