 { White has the superior centre, but lags behind in development. Find the best sequence for Black. } 1... { -KEY- } f6 $1 ( 1... c5 $6 { is much less constructive, since now White has time to consolidate the centre after } 2. f4 $1 Nc6 3. Nf3 $16 ) 
2. f4 $2 { White tries to hold the central pawn. } ( { White should have played } 2. exf6 { , but after } Qxf6 $11 { the position becomes equal as neither side dominates the centre. } ) 
fxe5 $1 3. dxe5 ( { If } 3. fxe5 { then after } Qh4 $19 { White is in trouble since they cannot play g2-g3 without losing a rook after ...Qe4+. } ) 
Nxe5 $1 { The final piece of the puzzle. Black grabs the pawn in order to expose the enemy king. } 4. fxe5 $2 { Best was to not capture the knight, but continuing to play a clean pawn down is not a fun prospect. } Qh4+ 5. g3 ( { If } 5. Kd2 { then } Qf4 $1 { wins the piece back with interest. } ) 
Qe4+ { Correct! This was one of the hardest puzzles in the course so far in my opinion, well done if you solved it correctly from beginning to end. } 
